## What

Adds a design doc, `docs/design_docs/nvfp4_msa_paged_kv_layout.md`, that consolidates the physical layout of the NVFP4 paged-KV cache consumed by the MSA decode and prefill routes in `flashinfer.msa_ops`.

Today the layout lives as inline comments and the `page_layout()` / `_v_scale_unswizzle_index()` helpers. This doc gathers it into one referenceable place:

- fixed geometry (D=128, P=128, G=16, per-rank `Hkv`);
- E2M1 data / E4M3 block-scale encoding and the explicit per-side global scales;
- the planar page map (`[K data | K scale | V data | V scale]`, 73,728 B at Hkv=4) that `page_layout()` returns;
- the vLLM SM100 (4,4) V-scale swizzle that `_v_scale_unswizzle_index()` inverts, with the exact index formula;
- the four strided public views and the byte-offset check the decode route runs before serving a call;
- how the vLLM producer and the reader spell the same bytes (the `[pages, 2, Hkv, P, 72]` form is an allocation alias);
- the MiniMax `nv_dev` adapter (separate contiguous tensors, linear V scales, token-major top-k, FP8 Q);
- the selection/position contract (per-token independent top-k, right-aligned positions, tail masking by `seqused_k`);
- a reader validation checklist, including the scale-free per-row relative-L2 metric the NVFP4 decode tests already use (an absolute tolerance is blind because a softmax-weighted mean of V has magnitude ~|V|/sqrt(N)).

## Why

The layout is a hard ABI shared by the block-scale writer, the decode/prefill readers, and external producers. A single accurate reference makes it easier to add readers, validate a new producer, and reason about the byte-offset guard.

## Scope

Documentation only. It references existing public symbols and adds no code. The described geometry, swizzle formula, and byte offsets were checked against the current `flashinfer/msa_ops/_nvfp4_decode_sm100.py`.

